~ A live HTTP GET with the `core.http` client: `get` builds the request, sends it over
~ core.net's `@tcpRequest`, and parses the reply into `Ok(Response)`. We assert the request
~ succeeded, the status is 200, and the body is the expected page — then that a refused
~ connection surfaces as `NotOk` rather than crashing. On success the program exits 0.
~ NOTE: this makes a LIVE network call to example.com:80 when run.
<< core.http

^ = () -> $ => <
  result = get("http://example.com/")
  assert(result, isOk())
  response = result ?
    | Ok(page) => page
    | NotOk(_) => Response { raw = "" }
  assert(response.status(), equals(200))
  assert(response.body(), contains("Example Domain"))

  ~ The reply carries headers, read case-insensitively off the live response.
  assert(response.header("Content-Type"), isOk())

  ~ A transport failure comes back as NotOk, not a crash.
  assert(get("http://127.0.0.1:1/"), isNotOk())
>
